    "id": "thm:one-group-benchmark",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For absolute constants \\(0<c<C<\\infty\\), \\(c\\min\\{1,K\\log(eK)/n\\}\\le R(n,1,K)\\le C\\min\\{1,K\\log(eK)/n\\}\\) for every \\(n\\ge1\\) and \\(K\\ge2\\); this adds the global bounded-Bernoulli all-estimator converse absent from the instance-dependent upper-bound theory of Nguyen, Barrett, and Jun, while leaving their easy-instance guarantees intact.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(n\\)",
      "\\(K\\)",
      "\\(R(n,d,K)\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "lem:empirical-upper",
      "lem:centered-winner-lower",
      "lem:haver-upper-bound-scope"
    ],
    "justification": "The passive one-group slice fixes the unavoidable \\(\\log K\\) factor and prevents importing adaptive best-arm sample complexity.",
    "gap": "NguyenBarrettJun2025HAVER proves sharp instance-dependent MSE upper bounds but no matching global minimax lower bound over bounded Bernoulli means.",
    "consumer": "Maximum-mean estimation in Q-learning and Monte Carlo tree search gains a finite-sample worst-case benchmark for passive data.",
    "proof_tex": "Set \\(d=1\\) in \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}empirical\\text{-}upper}\\) and \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}centered\\text{-}winner\\text{-}lower}\\).  The resulting upper scale is \\(K\\log(eK)\\), while the lower scale is \\(K\\log(1+\\sqrt K)\\).  For \\(K\\geq2\\),\n\\[\n \\log(1+\\sqrt K)\\geq\\frac12\\log K\n \\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n \\log(eK)=1+\\log K<\\frac52\\log K.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\log(1+\\sqrt K)\\geq\\frac15\\log(eK)\\).  The elementary relation \\(\\min\\{1,\\rho z\\}\\geq\\rho\\min\\{1,z\\}\\) for \\(0<\\rho\\leq1\\) proves the two-sided benchmark with absolute constants.  The bibliographic scope assertion in the final clause is exactly the delivery distinction recorded in \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}haver\\text{-}upper\\text{-}bound\\text{-}scope}\\): that work provides estimator-specific MSE upper bounds, whereas the lower inequality just proved quantifies over all estimators on the bounded-Bernoulli class.  Its easy-instance upper guarantees are not altered by this converse.",
